IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ddtr-dates`

-- Dust Diseases Tribunal Regulation 2019 (NSW), Part 3 -- the claims resolution process for asbestos-related
-- conditions, from the seat of the person who claims: the plaintiff who is or was suffering from the asbestos-related
-- condition, or a person claiming through them (including a Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 claim), and, after the
-- plaintiff's death, the authorised representative.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant (the plaintiff). Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Is my claim malignant?",
-- "Is my claim in the claims resolution process?", "Is my claim urgent?", "By when must each step be done?", "Did I
-- notify the registrar in time?", "Have I given the defendant the further information in time?", "Must I go to the
-- defendant's medical examination?", "Can I refuse the defendant more time for its cross-claims?", "Must I be at the
-- mediation?", "Can I be made to give evidence to the mediator?", "Who pays for the mediation?". The Tribunal, the
-- registrar, the mediator and the Contributions Assessor get no seat: their determinations, satisfaction and
-- discretions are inputs, answered conditionally.
--
-- Provisions: cl 11 (definitions), 13, 16(1)-(6), 17(3), (4), 18(1), (2), 20(1)-(5), 23(5), 25(2)-(5), (7), (8), 26(4), (5),
-- (8), 28(1), (2), 29(1)-(5), 30, 34(1), (2), (6), (7), 35(1), (3), (4), 36(7), 39(3), 41, 42(1), (4)-(6), (10),
-- 46(1), (2), 49(1), (4), 51(2), 52(7), (9), 54(4),
-- (5), 67(3)(g), 100(3)-(5). Out, with reasons in NOTES.md: the defendants' own machinery (cross-claim mechanics, replies,
-- apportionment among themselves, the single claims manager, Division 6), the mediator's and registrar's own powers, and
-- privilege (cl 48).
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the text says no in its own words (a "means"
-- definition, a condition introduced by "if", "only if", "unless", "until", a period the text fixes, a flat "must not",
-- "does not apply"), or NOTHING where it does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind"
-- comment.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website at 10 March 2025, via the Open Australian
-- Legal Corpus (version as at 1 July 2022). State of New South Wales, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE
-- ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Dust Diseases Tribunal Regulation 2019, Part 3 -- the claims resolution process`

§§ `The nouns`

-- cl 11(1) "asbestos-related condition means a dust-related condition that is asbestosis, asbestos-induced carcinoma, an
-- asbestos-related pleural disease or mesothelioma." "malignant claim means a claim in respect of asbestos-induced
-- carcinoma or mesothelioma (whether or not the claim is also in respect of any other dust-related condition)."
-- "non-malignant claim means a claim in respect of an asbestos-related condition that is not asbestos-induced carcinoma
-- or mesothelioma (and that is not a malignant claim)."
DECLARE `The conditions my claim is about` IS ONE OF
    `mesothelioma or asbestos-induced carcinoma, with or without another condition`
    `asbestosis or an asbestos-related pleural disease, and neither mesothelioma nor asbestos-induced carcinoma`
    `a dust-related condition that is not asbestos-related`
    `a condition that is not dust-related`

DECLARE `My claim for the timetable` IS ONE OF
    `a malignant claim`
    `a non-malignant claim`
    `a non-malignant claim treated as a malignant claim`
    `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`

-- cl 13(1): who makes the claim.
DECLARE `Who makes the claim` IS ONE OF
    `the person who is or was suffering from the condition`
    `a person claiming through them, including a compensation to relatives claim`
    `someone else`

-- The steps whose periods Part 3 fixes, each counted in business days after service of the plaintiff's statement of
-- particulars on the last of the original defendants (cl 14), except an original defendant's reply (cl 26(4)), which
-- is counted from service of the claim on that defendant.
DECLARE `The step in my claim` IS ONE OF
    `cross-claims filed and served (cl 25(2))`
    `the reply of an original defendant (cl 26(4))`
    `the reply of any other defendant or cross-defendant (cl 26(5))`
    `notice that a defendant requires a medical examination (cl 29(1))`
    `the medical examination (cl 29(2))`
    `the defendants' agreement on apportionment (cl 51(2))`
    `the Contributions Assessor's determination (cl 52(7))`
    `referral for mediation (cl 34(1))`
    `conclusion of mediation (cl 35(1))`

-- The notices the plaintiff must give the registrar, each with its period in business days.
DECLARE `The notice I must give the registrar` IS ONE OF
    `the date my statement of particulars was served on the last original defendant (cl 23(5))`
    `an agreement to extend or suspend the claims resolution process (cl 16(6))`
    `the claim being referred for mediation (cl 34(2)(a))`
    `the claim being settled (cl 34(2)(b))`

-- cl 30(1): why a party changes the facts it relies on.
DECLARE `Why I am changing my facts` IS ONE OF
    `I discovered facts I did not know and could not reasonably have discovered before serving`
    `I remembered facts only after serving my statement of particulars`
    `another party changed the facts it relies on`
    `some other reason`

DECLARE `Whether I may change my facts` IS ONE OF
    `yes: clause 30 authorises the change; notify the other parties as soon as practicable (cl 30(4))`
    `you can, but the change is not authorised by clause 30 and is a contravention the Tribunal must weigh in costs (cl 30(5), 69)`
    `clause 30 does not yet apply: you have not served your statement of particulars`

-- cl 25(3)-(5): a defendant's request for more time for cross-claims.
DECLARE `What I can do about the request for more time for cross-claims` IS ONE OF
    `you are taken to consent: you cannot show the extension would substantially prejudice you (cl 25(5))`
    `you may refuse: you can show the extension would substantially prejudice you (cl 25(5))`
    `the request came too late: it can only be made before the period ends (cl 25(3))`
    `the extension cannot be given: the total would exceed the cap (cl 25(4))`
    `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`

-- cl 20(2), (3), (5): an application by the claimant that the claim is urgent.
DECLARE `What the Tribunal must do with my urgency application` IS ONE OF
    `determine the claim urgent: it leaves the claims resolution process (cl 20(1)(a))`
    `instead of determining it urgent, order that it be treated as a malignant claim (cl 20(3))`
    `the claim is not urgent (cl 20(2): urgent only if)`
    `the application must not be made yet: the parties must first use their best efforts to agree to treat it as malignant (cl 20(5))`
    `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`

-- cl 42: evidence before the mediator about contribution.
DECLARE `Whether I can be made to give evidence to the mediator` IS ONE OF
    `yes, if the mediator requires it on the defendant's application; the requesting defendants pay its costs (cl 42(1), (10))`
    `yes, if the mediator requires it; the defendant's notice was late, a failure the Tribunal weighs in costs (cl 42(5), 69)`
    `no: clause 42 applies only after mediation results in settlement`
    `no: the defendant has not given notice to the mediator, you and each other party (cl 42(4))`
    `no: no defendant intends to dispute its contribution (cl 42(1))`

-- cl 49(1): costs of mediation.
DECLARE `Who bears the costs of the mediation` IS ONE OF
    `the parties, in the proportions they agree among themselves (cl 49(1)(a))`
    `the defendant, or all the defendants in equal shares; not you (cl 49(1)(b))`

-- The regulative rules' parties and acts.
DECLARE `Claims process party` IS ONE OF
    `the plaintiff`
    `the defendant`
    `the authorised representative`

DECLARE `Claims process act` IS ONE OF
    `notify the registrar`
    `give the further information the defendant reasonably required`
    `notify the plaintiff that a medical examination is required`
    `hold the medical examination`

§§ `cl 11 -- malignant and non-malignant claims; cl 20(3), (4) -- treated as malignant`

-- cl 20(3): the Tribunal must, instead of determining a non-malignant claim urgent, order that it be treated as a
-- malignant claim, if it would not be urgent on that footing. cl 20(4): a non-malignant claim is also treated as
-- malignant if all the parties agree and notify the registrar.
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about`
      `the Tribunal ordered it treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `all parties agreed it be treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `My claim for the timetable`
`my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` `the Tribunal ordered it treated as malignant` `all parties agreed it be treated as malignant` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my conditions`
    WHEN `mesothelioma or asbestos-induced carcinoma, with or without another condition` THEN `a malignant claim`
    WHEN `asbestosis or an asbestos-related pleural disease, and neither mesothelioma nor asbestos-induced carcinoma` THEN
        IF `the Tribunal ordered it treated as malignant` OR `all parties agreed it be treated as malignant`
        THEN `a non-malignant claim treated as a malignant claim`
        ELSE `a non-malignant claim`
    OTHERWISE `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`

GIVEN c IS A `My claim for the timetable`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`runs on the malignant timetable` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `a malignant claim` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a non-malignant claim treated as a malignant claim` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN c IS A `My claim for the timetable`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a Part 3 claim` c MEANS
    CONSIDER c
    WHEN `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Is my asbestos claim a malignant or a non-malignant claim for the Dust Diseases Tribunal's claims resolution process, and is it treated as malignant (cl 11, 20(3), (4))?
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about: mesothelioma or asbestos-induced carcinoma (with or without others); asbestosis or an asbestos-related pleural disease only; another dust-related condition; or a condition not related to dust
      `the Tribunal ordered it treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal, on an urgency application, ordered that your non-malignant claim be treated as a malignant claim (cl 20(3))
      `all parties agreed it be treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties agreed that your non-malignant claim be treated as a malignant claim and told the registrar (cl 20(4))
GIVETH A `My claim for the timetable`
`Is my claim malignant or non-malignant` MEANS
    `my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` `the Tribunal ordered it treated as malignant` `all parties agreed it be treated as malignant`

§§ `cl 13, 16, 20(1), 100(3)-(5) -- is my claim in the claims resolution process`

-- cl 13(1) "This Part applies to a claim in respect of an asbestos-related condition that is made by the person who is or
-- was suffering from the asbestos-related condition (or by a person claiming through that person ...)". cl 16(1) "subject
-- to the claims resolution process once the claim is filed"; (2) "remains subject ... (a) until the claim is settled ...
-- and the Tribunal makes an order to give effect to the settlement, or (b) if the claim is not settled by the time
-- mediation is concluded--until mediation is concluded and clause 40 has been complied with"; (3) the parties can agree
-- to extend. cl 20(1) "A claim is removed from (and is therefore not subject to) the claims resolution process if" (a)
-- urgent, (b) all agree after the exchange, (c) another party's failure, (d) 12 months without progress. cl 100(3) "A
-- provision of this Regulation extends to a claim that is pending immediately before the commencement of this
-- Regulation, except as otherwise provided by this clause." cl 100(4) kept Part 3 off claims pending on 1 September 2019,
-- but (5) "Subclause (4) ceases to have effect on 1 March 2020. However, if on 1 March 2020 any of the following
-- processes have commenced under the provisions of Part 4 of the repealed Regulation and have not concluded, that Part
-- continues to apply to that process until its conclusion-- (a) a mediation, (b) an apportionment process". The question
-- is asked today, so cl 100(4) no longer excludes anything; only a 2013 mediation or apportionment still running is
-- carved out. cl 20(8) (return after the plaintiff's death) is in NOTES.md, not here.
@export Is my claim in the Dust Diseases Tribunal's claims resolution process for asbestos-related conditions right now (cl 13, 16, 20, 100(3)-(5))?
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `who makes the claim` IS A `Who makes the claim` @desc Who brings the claim: the person who has (or had) the condition; a person claiming through them (for example their estate or relatives under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897); or someone else
      `my claim has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your statement of claim has been filed in the Tribunal
      `my claim was pending before 1 September 2019` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim was already pending in the Tribunal immediately before 1 September 2019
      `a 2013 Regulation mediation or apportionment is still running` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a claim pending before 1 September 2019: whether a mediation or an apportionment process begun under Part 4 of the Dust Diseases Tribunal Regulation 2013 before 1 March 2020 has still not concluded
      `the Tribunal determined my claim urgent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal, on your application, has determined that your claim is urgent (cl 20(1)(a))
      `all parties agreed to take it out of the process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties agreed, after the required information exchange, that the claim should not be in the process, and told the registrar (cl 20(1)(b))
      `the Tribunal removed it for a failure or for inactivity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal removed the claim because a party failed to comply with the process, or because nothing was filed and the claim did not progress for 12 months (cl 20(1)(c), (d))
      `my claim has been settled and the Tribunal made an order giving effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim was settled (at mediation or otherwise) and the Tribunal has made an order to give effect to the settlement
      `mediation has concluded without settlement and clause 40 is complied with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether mediation has concluded without settlement and the issues in dispute have been agreed and certified or each party has lodged its statement of issues (cl 40)
      `the parties agreed to extend the process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed to extend the time the claim remains in the process (cl 16(3))
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my claim in the claims resolution process` MEANS
    IF NOT (`is a Part 3 claim` (`my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` FALSE FALSE))
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 13 "This Part applies only to asbestos-related claims" (heading; a "means" definition of the condition)
    ELSE CONSIDER `who makes the claim`
         WHEN `someone else` THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 13(1) names who makes the claim (fork F1)
         OTHERWISE
             IF `my claim was pending before 1 September 2019` AND `a 2013 Regulation mediation or apportionment is still running`
             THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 100(5): Part 4 of the 2013 Regulation "continues to apply to that process until its conclusion"; the text does not say whether Part 3 governs the rest of the claim meanwhile; fork F16)
             ELSE IF NOT `my claim has been filed`
                  THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 16(1) "once the claim is filed": a "when" condition, not yet met
                  ELSE IF `the Tribunal determined my claim urgent` OR `all parties agreed to take it out of the process` OR `the Tribunal removed it for a failure or for inactivity`
                       THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 20(1) "is removed from (and is therefore not subject to)"
                       ELSE IF (`my claim has been settled and the Tribunal made an order giving effect` OR `mediation has concluded without settlement and clause 40 is complied with`) AND (NOT `the parties agreed to extend the process`)
                            THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 16(2) "remains ... until": the period the text fixes has ended
                            ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `cl 20(2)-(5) -- is my claim urgent`

-- cl 20(2) "A claim is urgent only if the Tribunal is satisfied that, as a result of the seriousness of the claimant's
-- medical condition-- (a) the claimant's life expectancy is so short as to leave insufficient time for the requirements
-- of the claims resolution process to be completed and the claim finally determined ... or (b) the claimant is not
-- likely to be able to give oral evidence or participate in Tribunal processes once the claims resolution process is
-- completed." (3) a non-malignant claim that would not be urgent if treated as malignant is ordered treated as
-- malignant instead. (5) "An application ... that a non-malignant claim is urgent must not be made until the parties
-- have used their best efforts to reach agreement as provided by subclause (4)."
@export If I apply to the Tribunal to have my asbestos claim treated as urgent, what must the Tribunal do (cl 20(2), (3), (5))?
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `the Tribunal is satisfied my life expectancy is too short` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied that, because of how serious your condition is, your life expectancy is too short for the process to be completed and the claim decided, if need be on an expedited basis
      `the Tribunal is satisfied I will not be able to give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied that, because of how serious your condition is, you are not likely to be able to give oral evidence or take part once the process is completed
      `the Tribunal is satisfied it would still be urgent on the malignant timetable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a non-malignant claim: whether the Tribunal is satisfied the claim would still be urgent even if it were run on the shorter malignant timetable
      `the parties have used best efforts to agree to treat it as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a non-malignant claim: whether the parties have first used their best efforts to agree that it be treated as a malignant claim (cl 20(4), (5))
GIVETH A `What the Tribunal must do with my urgency application`
`Is my claim urgent` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my conditions`
    WHEN `mesothelioma or asbestos-induced carcinoma, with or without another condition` THEN
        IF `the satisfaction cl 20(2) needs`
        THEN `determine the claim urgent: it leaves the claims resolution process (cl 20(1)(a))`
        ELSE `the claim is not urgent (cl 20(2): urgent only if)`
    WHEN `asbestosis or an asbestos-related pleural disease, and neither mesothelioma nor asbestos-induced carcinoma` THEN
        IF NOT `the parties have used best efforts to agree to treat it as malignant`
        THEN `the application must not be made yet: the parties must first use their best efforts to agree to treat it as malignant (cl 20(5))`
        ELSE IF NOT `the satisfaction cl 20(2) needs`
             THEN `the claim is not urgent (cl 20(2): urgent only if)`
             ELSE IF `the Tribunal is satisfied it would still be urgent on the malignant timetable`
                  THEN `determine the claim urgent: it leaves the claims resolution process (cl 20(1)(a))`
                  ELSE `instead of determining it urgent, order that it be treated as a malignant claim (cl 20(3))`
    OTHERWISE `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`
    WHERE
        `the satisfaction cl 20(2) needs` MEANS `the Tribunal is satisfied my life expectancy is too short` OR `the Tribunal is satisfied I will not be able to give evidence`

§§ `The timetable -- cl 25, 26, 29, 34, 35, 51, 52, with extensions`

-- The base periods, in business days, as the text fixes them:
--   cl 25(2) cross-claims: 10 malignant, 30 non-malignant.
--   cl 26(4) an original defendant's reply: 20 / 30 after the claim is served on it.
--   cl 26(5) any other defendant's reply: 30 / 60.
--   cl 29(1) notice of a medical examination: 10 / 20 malignant (one / more than one defendant), 30 / 50 non-malignant.
--   cl 29(2) the medical examination: 20 / 30 malignant, 40 / 60 non-malignant.
--   cl 51(2) agreement on apportionment: 35 / 70.
--   cl 52(7) the Contributions Assessor's determination: 40 / 80.
--   cl 34(1) referral for mediation: 30 / 50 malignant, 60 / 100 non-malignant.
--   cl 35(1) conclusion of mediation: 45 / 60 malignant, 90 / 120 non-malignant.
GIVEN step IS A `The step in my claim`, malignant IS A BOOLEAN, `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the base period in business days for` step malignant `more than one defendant` MEANS
    CONSIDER step
    WHEN `cross-claims filed and served (cl 25(2))` THEN `by kind` 10 30
    WHEN `the reply of an original defendant (cl 26(4))` THEN `by kind` 20 30
    WHEN `the reply of any other defendant or cross-defendant (cl 26(5))` THEN `by kind` 30 60
    WHEN `notice that a defendant requires a medical examination (cl 29(1))` THEN `by kind and number` 10 20 30 50
    WHEN `the medical examination (cl 29(2))` THEN `by kind and number` 20 30 40 60
    WHEN `the defendants' agreement on apportionment (cl 51(2))` THEN `by kind` 35 70
    WHEN `the Contributions Assessor's determination (cl 52(7))` THEN `by kind` 40 80
    WHEN `referral for mediation (cl 34(1))` THEN `by kind and number` 30 50 60 100
    WHEN `conclusion of mediation (cl 35(1))` THEN `by kind and number` 45 60 90 120
    WHERE
        `by kind` a b MEANS IF malignant THEN a ELSE b
        `by kind and number` a b c d MEANS
            IF malignant
            THEN IF `more than one defendant` THEN b ELSE a
            ELSE IF `more than one defendant` THEN d ELSE c

-- cl 25(7) "An extension of the time for filing and serving a cross-claim extends (for the period of the extension) all
-- subsequent time periods". Read here as: the cross-claim period itself and every period that ends later than the
-- cross-claim period; an original defendant's reply, counted from a different day, is not extended (fork F4).
GIVEN step IS A `The step in my claim`, malignant IS A BOOLEAN, `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is extended by a cross-claim extension` step malignant `more than one defendant` MEANS
    CONSIDER step
    WHEN `cross-claims filed and served (cl 25(2))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the reply of an original defendant (cl 26(4))` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE
            `the base period in business days for` step malignant `more than one defendant`
        GREATER THAN `the base period in business days for` `cross-claims filed and served (cl 25(2))` malignant `more than one defendant`

-- The period for a step, with every extension the text gives:
--   cl 16(5), 18(1), 27(2): time does not run while the process is suspended (all periods);
--   cl 25(4), (7): a cross-claim extension, at most 10 (malignant) or 20 (non-malignant) business days in total;
--   cl 34(6), (7): one registrar deferral of referral, at most 5 / 20, added to the cl 35(1) period;
--   cl 35(3), (4): mediator deferrals, at most 5 / 20 each occasion, added to the cl 35(1) period;
--   cl 36(7): each revoked registrar appointment of a mediator adds 2 to the cl 35(1) period;
--   cl 54(4), (5): each re-referral to another Contributions Assessor after an objection adds 2 to the cl 34(1), 35(1)
--   and 52(7) periods.
-- A registrar deferral or cross-claim extension above its cap gives NOTHING: the text forbids it and does not say what
-- follows (fork F5).
GIVEN step IS A `The step in my claim`
      claim IS A `My claim for the timetable`
      `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `suspended business days` IS A NUMBER
      `cross-claim extension` IS A NUMBER
      `registrar deferral` IS A NUMBER
      `mediator deferrals` IS A NUMBER
      `revoked mediators` IS A NUMBER
      `Assessor objections` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the period in business days for` step claim `more than one defendant` `suspended business days` `cross-claim extension` `registrar deferral` `mediator deferrals` `revoked mediators` `Assessor objections` MEANS
    IF NOT (`is a Part 3 claim` claim)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 13: Part 3 fixes no timetable for the claim)
    ELSE IF `cross-claim extension` GREATER THAN `the cap` 10 20 OR `registrar deferral` GREATER THAN `the cap` 5 20
         THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 25(4), 34(6) forbid it; the text does not say what an over-long one does; fork F5)
         ELSE JUST (`the base` PLUS `suspended business days` PLUS `the cross-claim part` PLUS `the mediation part` PLUS `the Assessor part`)
    WHERE
        malignant MEANS `runs on the malignant timetable` claim
        `the cap` a b MEANS IF malignant THEN a ELSE b
        `the base` MEANS `the base period in business days for` step malignant `more than one defendant`
        `the cross-claim part` MEANS
            IF `is extended by a cross-claim extension` step malignant `more than one defendant`
            THEN `cross-claim extension`
            ELSE 0
        `the mediation part` MEANS
            CONSIDER step
            WHEN `conclusion of mediation (cl 35(1))` THEN `registrar deferral` PLUS `mediator deferrals` PLUS (2 TIMES `revoked mediators`)
            OTHERWISE 0
        `the Assessor part` MEANS
            CONSIDER step
            WHEN `referral for mediation (cl 34(1))` THEN 2 TIMES `Assessor objections`
            WHEN `conclusion of mediation (cl 35(1))` THEN 2 TIMES `Assessor objections`
            WHEN `the Contributions Assessor's determination (cl 52(7))` THEN 2 TIMES `Assessor objections`
            OTHERWISE 0

@export How many business days does the claims resolution process allow for this step in my asbestos claim, with any extensions (cl 25, 26, 29, 34, 35, 51, 52)?
GIVEN step IS A `The step in my claim` @desc Which step: cross-claims; an original defendant's reply; another defendant's reply; notice of a medical examination; the medical examination; the defendants' agreement on apportionment; the Contributions Assessor's determination; referral for mediation; conclusion of mediation
      `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `my claim is treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your non-malignant claim is treated as malignant, by the Tribunal's order or the parties' agreement (cl 20(3), (4))
      `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim involves more than one defendant (a cross-claim against another party counts, cl 25(13))
      `suspended business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the process has been suspended (by the parties' agreement after a significant change in your condition, at most 10 business days for a malignant claim or 20 for a non-malignant claim each time (cl 16(4)); after a death; or after a new defendant was joined), in total; 0 if none
      `cross-claim extension` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the time for cross-claims was extended, in total; 0 if none
      `registrar deferral` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the registrar deferred referring the claim for mediation, with everyone's agreement; 0 if none
      `mediator deferrals` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the mediator deferred the mediation, in total; 0 if none
      `revoked mediators` IS A NUMBER @desc How many times a mediator appointed by the registrar had the appointment revoked; 0 if none
      `Assessor objections` IS A NUMBER @desc How many times the apportionment was referred to another Contributions Assessor after an objection; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How many business days does this step have` MEANS
    `the period in business days for` step (`my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` `my claim is treated as malignant` FALSE) `more than one defendant` `suspended business days` `cross-claim extension` `registrar deferral` `mediator deferrals` `revoked mediators` `Assessor objections`

@export By what date must this step in my asbestos claim be done under the claims resolution process (cl 11(1), (2), 14, 25, 26, 29, 34, 35, 51, 52)?
GIVEN step IS A `The step in my claim` @desc Which step in the process
      `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `my claim is treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your non-malignant claim is treated as malignant (cl 20(3), (4))
      `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim involves more than one defendant (a cross-claim counts, cl 25(13))
      `the day my particulars reached the last original defendant` IS A DATE @desc The day your statement of particulars was served on the only original defendant, or on the last of them to be served (cl 14)
      `the day my claim was served on that original defendant` IS A DATE @desc Only for an original defendant's reply: the day your claim was served on that defendant (otherwise any date)
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2)); normally they do not count
      `weekday public holidays in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday in the period (leave out those between 25 December and 9 January unless they count); 0 if none
      `suspended business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the process has been suspended; 0 if none
      `cross-claim extension` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the time for cross-claims was extended, in total; 0 if none
      `registrar deferral` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the registrar deferred the referral for mediation; 0 if none
      `mediator deferrals` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the mediator deferred the mediation, in total; 0 if none
      `revoked mediators` IS A NUMBER @desc How many times a registrar-appointed mediator's appointment was revoked; 0 if none
      `Assessor objections` IS A NUMBER @desc How many times the apportionment went to another Contributions Assessor after an objection; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must this step be done` MEANS
    CONSIDER `How many business days does this step have` step `my conditions` `my claim is treated as malignant` `more than one defendant` `suspended business days` `cross-claim extension` `registrar deferral` `mediator deferrals` `revoked mediators` `Assessor objections`
    WHEN JUST n THEN JUST (`the end of` (n PLUS `weekday public holidays in the period`) `business days after` `the start` `ignoring Christmas` (NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`))
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (no period: see the business-days question)
    WHERE
        `the start` MEANS
            CONSIDER step
            WHEN `the reply of an original defendant (cl 26(4))` THEN `the day my claim was served on that original defendant`
            OTHERWISE `the day my particulars reached the last original defendant`

§§ `cl 16(6), 23(5), 34(2) -- the plaintiff's notices to the registrar`

-- cl 23(5) "Within 5 business days after serving the plaintiff's statement of particulars on a defendant, the plaintiff
-- must notify the registrar and each of the original defendants of the date on which the statement of particulars was
-- served on the last of the original defendants." cl 16(6) "Within 5 business days after any agreement is made under this
-- clause, the plaintiff must notify the registrar in writing of the agreement." cl 34(2) "The plaintiff must, within one
-- business day, notify the registrar in writing of ... (a) the claim being referred for mediation, or (b) the claim being
-- settled (unless an application for a consent judgment or order has been filed)." A failure is weighed in costs (cl 69)
-- and its consequences are costs of mediation (cl 49(4)(b)).
GIVEN `business days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claims process party` `Claims process act`
`the plaintiff must notify the registrar within` `business days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff`
    MUST `notify the registrar`
    WITHIN `business days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff` BECAUSE "the registrar was not notified within the time (cl 16(6), 23(5) or 34(2)); a failure the Tribunal must take into account in costs (cl 69)"

GIVEN notice IS A `The notice I must give the registrar`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the business days for` notice MEANS
    CONSIDER notice
    WHEN `the claim being referred for mediation (cl 34(2)(a))` THEN 1
    WHEN `the claim being settled (cl 34(2)(b))` THEN 1
    OTHERWISE 5

@export Did I (or will I, if I notify on this day) give the registrar this notice in time under the claims resolution process (cl 16(6), 23(5), 34(2))?
GIVEN notice IS A `The notice I must give the registrar` @desc Which notice: the date your statement of particulars reached the last original defendant; an agreement to extend or suspend the process; the claim being referred for mediation; the claim being settled
      `the day of the event` IS A DATE @desc The day of the event the notice is about: the day you served your statement of particulars on the last original defendant, the day the agreement was made, the day the claim was referred for mediation, or the day it settled
      `the day I notify` IS A DATE @desc The day you notified (or will notify) the registrar in writing
      `a consent judgment application has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for a settlement: whether an application for a consent judgment or order has been filed
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2))
      `weekday public holidays in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday between the event and the day you notify; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my notice to the registrar in time` MEANS
    IF `the settlement notice is excused`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 34(2)(b) "unless" met: no notice is required; fork F6)
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the plaintiff must notify the registrar within` (`the business days for` notice PLUS `weekday public holidays in the period`)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the plaintiff` `notify the registrar` `the notice time`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- the period the text fixes has passed
    WHERE
        `the settlement notice is excused` MEANS
            CONSIDER notice
            WHEN `the claim being settled (cl 34(2)(b))` THEN `a consent judgment application has been filed`
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `the notice time` MEANS `the business days after` `the day of the event` `up to` `the day I notify` `ignoring Christmas` (NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`)

§§ `cl 26(8) -- further information the defendant requires; cl 28(2) -- information I request`

-- cl 26(8) "The plaintiff must comply with any reasonable requirement of the defendant for more information concerning the
-- plaintiff's claim ... and must provide that information as soon as practicable and, in any case, no later than 5
-- business days before the start of mediation." The "as soon as practicable" limb is a judgment, not encoded; the outer
-- limit is. cl 28(2) puts the same limit on a defendant answering the plaintiff's request.
GIVEN `business days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claims process party` `Claims process act`
`the plaintiff must give the further information within` `business days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff`
    MUST `give the further information the defendant reasonably required`
    WITHIN `business days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff` BECAUSE "the further information was not given at least 5 business days before mediation started (cl 26(8)); a failure the Tribunal must take into account in costs (cl 69)"

@export The defendant asked me for more information about my claim: have I given it (or will I, on this day) by the latest day the claims resolution process allows (cl 26(8))?
GIVEN `the requirement is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant's requirement for more information is a reasonable one
      `the day the defendant required it` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant made the requirement (in its reply or later)
      `the day mediation starts` IS A DATE @desc The day the mediation starts
      `the day I give it` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave (or will give) the information
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2))
      `weekday public holidays in the 5 business days before mediation` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday in the days counted back from the mediation; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Have I given the further information in time` MEANS
    IF NOT `the requirement is reasonable`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 26(8) reaches only a reasonable requirement; fork F6)
    ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `the latest day` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL `the day the defendant required it`
         THEN JUST FALSE  -- the requirement came after the last day the text allows; no compliance can be in time (fork F7)
         ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the plaintiff must give the further information within` `the business days allowed`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the plaintiff` `give the further information the defendant reasonably required` `the giving time`))
              WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
              OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- the period the text fixes has passed
    WHERE
        `counting` MEANS NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`
        `the latest day` MEANS `the latest day` (5 PLUS `weekday public holidays in the 5 business days before mediation`) `business days before` `the day mediation starts` `ignoring Christmas` `counting`
        `the business days allowed` MEANS `the business days after` `the day the defendant required it` `up to` `the latest day` `ignoring Christmas` `counting`
        `the giving time` MEANS `the business days after` `the day the defendant required it` `up to` `the day I give it` `ignoring Christmas` `counting`

@export I asked a defendant for the information it relies on for an issue it does not admit: by what date must it give it to me at the latest (cl 28)?
GIVEN `the day mediation starts` IS A DATE @desc The day the mediation starts
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2))
      `weekday public holidays in the 5 business days before mediation` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday in the days counted back from the mediation; 0 if none
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defendant answer my request` MEANS
    `the latest day` (5 PLUS `weekday public holidays in the 5 business days before mediation`) `business days before` `the day mediation starts` `ignoring Christmas` (NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`)

§§ `cl 29 -- medical examinations`

-- cl 29(1) an original defendant who requires the plaintiff to attend a medical examination "must notify the plaintiff
-- of that requirement within" 10 / 20 / 30 / 50 business days, and (2) the examination "must take place within" 20 / 30 /
-- 40 / 60 business days, both after service of the statement of particulars on the last original defendant. (3) all the
-- parties may vary the (2) period. A requirement outside the periods is not one cl 29 allows: JUST FALSE (a period the text
-- fixes). A cross-defendant cannot require an examination while the claim is in the process (the Note to cl 29: it needs
-- the Tribunal's leave afterwards); answered NOTHING here (fork F8).
GIVEN `notice days` IS A NUMBER, `exam days after notice` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claims process party` `Claims process act`
`the defendant's medical examination within` `notice days` `and then` `exam days after notice` MEANS
    PARTY `the defendant`
    MUST `notify the plaintiff that a medical examination is required`
    WITHIN `notice days`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the defendant`
        MUST `hold the medical examination`
        WITHIN `exam days after notice`
        LEST BREACH BY `the defendant` BECAUSE "the medical examination did not take place within the time (cl 29(2))"
    LEST BREACH BY `the defendant` BECAUSE "the plaintiff was not notified of the medical examination within the time (cl 29(1))"

@export Is a defendant's requirement that I attend its medical examination within the time limits of the claims resolution process (cl 29(1)-(3))?
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `my claim is treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your non-malignant claim is treated as malignant (cl 20(3), (4))
      `more than one defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim involves more than one defendant (a cross-claim counts)
      `the defendant is an original defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant requiring the examination was a defendant before anyone else was joined (not a cross-defendant)
      `the day my particulars reached the last original defendant` IS A DATE @desc The day your statement of particulars was served on the only or last original defendant
      `the day the defendant notified me` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant told you it requires the examination
      `the day of the examination` IS A DATE @desc The day the examination takes (or took) place
      `all parties agreed to vary the examination period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties agreed to change the period within which the examination must take place (cl 29(3))
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2))
      `weekday public holidays before the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday after service of your particulars, up to and including the day the defendant notified you; 0 if none
      `weekday public holidays before the examination` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday after service of your particulars, up to and including the day of the examination; 0 if none
      `suspended business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the process was suspended; 0 if none
      `cross-claim extension` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the time for cross-claims was extended, in total; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the medical examination requirement in time` MEANS
    IF NOT `the defendant is an original defendant`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 29 speaks only of original defendants; fork F8)
    ELSE CONSIDER `the notice period`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 13: not a Part 3 claim, or an over-long extension)
         WHEN JUST `notice days` THEN
             CONSIDER `the examination period`
             WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (as above)
             WHEN JUST `exam days` THEN
                 CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the defendant's medical examination within` (`notice days` PLUS `weekday public holidays before the notice`) `and then` (`the examination limit` `exam days`)) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the defendant` `notify the plaintiff that a medical examination is required` `the notice time`), EVENT `the defendant` `hold the medical examination` `the examination time`)
                 WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
                 OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- a period the text fixes has passed
    WHERE
        `the claim` MEANS `my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` `my claim is treated as malignant` FALSE
        `counting` MEANS NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`
        `the notice period` MEANS `the period in business days for` `notice that a defendant requires a medical examination (cl 29(1))` `the claim` `more than one defendant` `suspended business days` `cross-claim extension` 0 0 0 0
        `the examination period` MEANS `the period in business days for` `the medical examination (cl 29(2))` `the claim` `more than one defendant` `suspended business days` `cross-claim extension` 0 0 0 0
        `the notice time` MEANS `the business days after` `the day my particulars reached the last original defendant` `up to` `the day the defendant notified me` `ignoring Christmas` `counting`
        `the examination time` MEANS `the business days after` `the day my particulars reached the last original defendant` `up to` `the day of the examination` `ignoring Christmas` `counting`
        `the examination limit` `exam days` MEANS
            IF `all parties agreed to vary the examination period`
            THEN (`the examination time` MINUS `the notice time`)
            ELSE (`exam days` PLUS `weekday public holidays before the examination` MINUS `the notice time`)

-- cl 29(4) "If more than one original defendant requires the plaintiff to attend for a medical examination ... the
-- plaintiff is entitled to choose to have those medical examinations conducted as a joint examination". (5) "Subclause
-- (4) does not apply to a medical examination required by a defendant if the defendant objects".
@export Several defendants want to examine me: can I choose to have this defendant's medical examination done as one joint examination (cl 29(4), (5))?
GIVEN `original defendants requiring an examination` IS A NUMBER @desc How many original defendants require you to attend a medical examination
      `this defendant objects to a joint examination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this defendant objects to its examination being part of a joint examination
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I choose a joint medical examination` MEANS
    IF `original defendants requiring an examination` AT MOST 1
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 29(4) "If more than one original defendant requires"
    ELSE IF `this defendant objects to a joint examination`
         THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 29(5) "does not apply ... if the defendant objects"
         ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `cl 25 -- the defendants' cross-claims: what the plaintiff can do`

-- cl 25(3) "An original defendant may request the plaintiff to extend the time ... but can only make the request before
-- the end of the period". (4) "cannot be extended in total by more than 10 business days for malignant claims or 20
-- business days for non-malignant claims". (5) "A plaintiff is taken to consent to a request to extend ... unless the
-- plaintiff is able to demonstrate that the extension requested would result in substantial prejudice to the plaintiff."
@export A defendant asked me for more time to file its cross-claims: can I refuse (cl 25(3)-(5))?
GIVEN `my conditions` IS A `The conditions my claim is about` @desc Which conditions your claim is about
      `my claim is treated as malignant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your non-malignant claim is treated as malignant (cl 20(3), (4))
      `the request came before the cross-claim period ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant asked before the time for filing and serving cross-claims ran out
      `business days already extended` IS A NUMBER @desc How many business days the time for cross-claims has already been extended; 0 if none
      `business days asked for` IS A NUMBER @desc How many more business days the defendant asks for
      `I can show substantial prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are able to demonstrate that the extension would cause you substantial prejudice
GIVETH A `What I can do about the request for more time for cross-claims`
`Can I refuse more time for cross-claims` MEANS
    IF NOT (`is a Part 3 claim` `the claim`)
    THEN `not a claim the claims resolution process applies to`
    ELSE IF NOT `the request came before the cross-claim period ended`
         THEN `the request came too late: it can only be made before the period ends (cl 25(3))`
         ELSE IF (`business days already extended` PLUS `business days asked for`) GREATER THAN `the cap`
              THEN `the extension cannot be given: the total would exceed the cap (cl 25(4))`
              ELSE IF `I can show substantial prejudice`
                   THEN `you may refuse: you can show the extension would substantially prejudice you (cl 25(5))`
                   ELSE `you are taken to consent: you cannot show the extension would substantially prejudice you (cl 25(5))`
    WHERE
        `the claim` MEANS `my claim for the timetable` `my conditions` `my claim is treated as malignant` FALSE
        `the cap` MEANS IF `runs on the malignant timetable` `the claim` THEN 10 ELSE 20

-- cl 25(8) "If a person is identified as the insurer of a defendant only after expiry of the period ... (a) a cross-claim
-- may, with the consent of the plaintiff, be filed and served on that insurer after the expiry of that period, and (b)
-- the plaintiff must not refuse that consent unless the plaintiff can establish that the joining of the insurer will
-- result in substantial prejudice to the plaintiff".
@export A defendant's insurer was identified only after the time for cross-claims ran out: may I refuse consent to its being joined late (cl 25(8))?
GIVEN `the insurer was identified only after the period expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person was identified as the defendant's insurer only after the time for cross-claims had run out
      `I can establish substantial prejudice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can establish that joining the insurer will cause you substantial prejudice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I refuse consent to joining the insurer` MEANS
    IF NOT `the insurer was identified only after the period expired`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 25(8) reaches only an insurer identified late)
    ELSE IF `I can establish substantial prejudice`
         THEN JUST TRUE  -- the "unless" is met
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- cl 25(8)(b) "must not refuse that consent unless"

§§ `cl 17(3), (4) -- amending my statement of claim`

-- cl 17(3) "while a claim is subject to the claims resolution process and before the plaintiff's statement of
-- particulars is served, the plaintiff may, without leave, amend the plaintiff's statement of claim." (4) "Subclause (3)
-- does not apply if the plaintiff's statement of claim is filed in conjunction with an application under clause 20 ...
-- and that application results in the making of a determination."
@export May I amend my statement of claim without the Tribunal's leave while my asbestos claim is in the claims resolution process (cl 17(3), (4))?
GIVEN `my claim is in the claims resolution process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is currently in the claims resolution process
      `my statement of particulars has been served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served your statement of particulars
      `my claim was filed with an urgency application that succeeded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your statement of claim was filed together with an urgency application that the Tribunal granted
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I amend my statement of claim without leave` MEANS
    IF `my claim was filed with an urgency application that succeeded`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 17(4) "Subclause (3) does not apply"
    ELSE IF `my claim is in the claims resolution process` AND (NOT `my statement of particulars has been served`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own words; outside it, rules of court decide)

§§ `cl 30 -- changing the facts I rely on`

-- cl 30(1) "After serving the party's statement of particulars or reply on a claim, a party ... may only change the facts
-- on which the party relies ... if the change is due to-- (a) the discovery ... of facts that were not known to and not
-- reasonably discoverable by the party before serving ..., or (b) the plaintiff remembering facts only after serving
-- the plaintiff's statement of particulars, or (c) a change by another party to the facts on which that other party
-- relies." (5) "This clause does not prevent a party changing the facts ..., but any change that is not authorised by
-- this clause constitutes a contravention of this clause for the purposes of clause 69."
@export After serving my statement of particulars, may I change the facts my asbestos claim relies on (cl 30)?
GIVEN `I have served my statement of particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served your statement of particulars
      `why I am changing my facts` IS A `Why I am changing my facts` @desc Why: facts you did not know and could not reasonably have found out before serving; facts you only remembered after serving; another party changed its facts; or some other reason
GIVETH A `Whether I may change my facts`
`May I change the facts I rely on` MEANS
    IF NOT `I have served my statement of particulars`
    THEN `clause 30 does not yet apply: you have not served your statement of particulars`
    ELSE CONSIDER `why I am changing my facts`
         WHEN `some other reason` THEN `you can, but the change is not authorised by clause 30 and is a contravention the Tribunal must weigh in costs (cl 30(5), 69)`
         OTHERWISE `yes: clause 30 authorises the change; notify the other parties as soon as practicable (cl 30(4))`

§§ `cl 39(3) -- being at the mediation`

-- cl 39(3) "The plaintiff must be present at a mediation session (either in person or by audio visual link) unless a
-- medical practitioner has certified that the plaintiff is not fit to be present."
@export Must I be present at the mediation of my asbestos claim, in person or by audio visual link (cl 39(3))?
GIVEN `my claim has been referred for mediation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim has been referred for mediation under the claims resolution process
      `a doctor certified I am not fit to be present` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical practitioner has certified that you are not fit to be present
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be present at the mediation` MEANS
    IF NOT `my claim has been referred for mediation`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (no mediation session under Part 3)
    ELSE IF `a doctor certified I am not fit to be present`
         THEN JUST FALSE  -- the "unless" is met
         ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `cl 42, 46(2) -- evidence before the mediator after a settlement`

-- cl 42(1) "If mediation of a claim results in settlement of the claim, the mediator may, on the application of a defendant
-- who intends to dispute in subsequent proceedings the contribution that the defendant is liable to make ..., require the
-- plaintiff to give evidence on oath before the mediator". (4) notice to the mediator, the plaintiff and each other party.
-- (5) "at least 2 business days before the start of mediation. If the decision ... is made after that ..., the notice may
-- still be given but this constitutes a failure to comply with this clause for the purposes of clause 69." (10) the
-- requesting defendant pays the costs of taking the evidence.
@export My asbestos claim settled at mediation: can a defendant have me give evidence on oath to the mediator about contribution (cl 42(1), (4), (5), (10))?
GIVEN `the mediation resulted in settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mediation resulted in settlement of your claim
      `a defendant intends to dispute its contribution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a defendant intends to dispute, in later proceedings, how much it must contribute to your damages
      `the defendant gave notice to the mediator, me and each other party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that defendant gave notice to the mediator, to you and to each other party that it wants you to give evidence
      `the day of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day that notice was given
      `the day mediation started` IS A DATE @desc The day the mediation started
      `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the parties agreed that the days from 25 December to 9 January are to count (cl 11(2))
      `weekday public holidays in the 2 business days before mediation` IS A NUMBER @desc How many public holidays throughout New South Wales fall on a weekday in the days counted back from the mediation; 0 if none
GIVETH A `Whether I can be made to give evidence to the mediator`
`Can I be made to give evidence to the mediator` MEANS
    IF NOT `the mediation resulted in settlement`
    THEN `no: clause 42 applies only after mediation results in settlement`
    ELSE IF NOT `a defendant intends to dispute its contribution`
         THEN `no: no defendant intends to dispute its contribution (cl 42(1))`
         ELSE IF NOT `the defendant gave notice to the mediator, me and each other party`
              THEN `no: the defendant has not given notice to the mediator, you and each other party (cl 42(4))`
              ELSE IF DATE_SERIAL `the day of the notice` AT MOST DATE_SERIAL `the latest day for notice`
                   THEN `yes, if the mediator requires it on the defendant's application; the requesting defendants pay its costs (cl 42(1), (10))`
                   ELSE `yes, if the mediator requires it; the defendant's notice was late, a failure the Tribunal weighs in costs (cl 42(5), 69)`
    WHERE
        `the latest day for notice` MEANS `the latest day` (2 PLUS `weekday public holidays in the 2 business days before mediation`) `business days before` `the day mediation started` `ignoring Christmas` (NOT `the parties agreed to count 25 December to 9 January`)

-- cl 42(6) "Once the plaintiff has given that evidence, the plaintiff cannot be required to give evidence in proceedings
-- before the Tribunal in respect of the dispute unless the Tribunal is satisfied that a failure by the plaintiff to give
-- the evidence concerned will cause substantial injustice to a defendant."
@export I gave evidence to the mediator about contribution: can I be required to give evidence again in the Tribunal on the defendants' contribution dispute (cl 42(6))?
GIVEN `I have given evidence to the mediator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already given evidence on oath before the mediator under clause 42
      `the Tribunal is satisfied of substantial injustice to a defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied that your not giving the evidence would cause substantial injustice to a defendant
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be required to give evidence again` MEANS
    IF NOT `I have given evidence to the mediator`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 42(6) speaks only once the evidence is given)
    ELSE IF `the Tribunal is satisfied of substantial injustice to a defendant`
         THEN JUST TRUE  -- the "unless" is met
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "cannot be required ... unless"

-- cl 46(2) "The Tribunal is not to make an order entering judgment in favour of the plaintiff if the plaintiff has failed
-- to provide any evidence that the plaintiff has been required to provide by the mediator under clause 42 and that failure
-- is continuing, unless the failure is due to the death or permanent incapacity of the plaintiff."
@export My asbestos claim settled at mediation: can the Tribunal enter judgment for me on the settlement if I have not given the evidence the mediator required (cl 46(2))?
GIVEN `the mediator required me to give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the mediator required you to give evidence under clause 42
      `my failure to give it is continuing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you still have not given that evidence
      `the failure is due to my death or permanent incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure is because the plaintiff has died or is permanently incapacitated
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can judgment be entered for me on the settlement` MEANS
    IF `the mediator required me to give evidence` AND `my failure to give it is continuing` AND (NOT `the failure is due to my death or permanent incapacity`)
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- "is not to make an order entering judgment ... if"
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 46(1) "may make orders": a power, not a duty; the Tribunal decides)

§§ `cl 49 -- costs of mediation`

-- cl 49(1) "The costs of mediation are to be borne by-- (a) the parties to the claim in the proportions as the parties may
-- agree among themselves if mediation results in settlement of the claim or the claim is otherwise settled, or (b) the
-- defendant (or all the defendants in equal shares) if mediation does not result in settlement of the claim and the
-- claim is not otherwise settled." (3) the Tribunal may still include them in a costs award, and may decline to if the
-- successful party did not take part in good faith. (4) "costs of mediation includes-- (a) remuneration payable to the
-- mediator, and (b) if the parties failed to notify the registrar under clause 34(2) ... any costs resulting from that
-- failure": so those costs follow cl 49(1) too, including when the plaintiff was the one who failed to notify.
@export Who pays the costs of the mediation of my asbestos claim (cl 49(1))?
GIVEN `my claim was settled, at mediation or otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim was settled, at the mediation or otherwise
GIVETH A `Who bears the costs of the mediation`
`Who pays for the mediation` MEANS
    IF `my claim was settled, at mediation or otherwise`
    THEN `the parties, in the proportions they agree among themselves (cl 49(1)(a))`
    ELSE `the defendant, or all the defendants in equal shares; not you (cl 49(1)(b))`

@export If my asbestos claim does not settle, what share of the mediation costs does each defendant bear (cl 49(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the costs of mediation` IS A NUMBER @desc The costs of the mediation in dollars (the mediator's remuneration, and any costs of a failure to notify the registrar)
      `number of defendants` IS A NUMBER @desc How many defendants there are (cross-defendants count)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What does each defendant pay for the mediation` MEANS
    IF `number of defendants` AT LEAST 1
    THEN JUST (`the costs of mediation` DIVIDED BY `number of defendants`)
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (no defendant)

§§ `cl 52(9) -- the Contributions Assessor's determination and me`

-- cl 52(9) "A determination of a Contributions Assessor under this Division cannot be challenged, reviewed, quashed or
-- called into question before any court ... This subclause-- (a) is not binding on the plaintiff, and (b) does not
-- change any law relating to causation as between the plaintiff and the defendants, and (c) does not displace any law
-- relating to the joint and several liability of the defendants".
@export Does the rule that a Contributions Assessor's determination cannot be challenged bind me as the plaintiff (cl 52(9))?
GIVEN `a Contributions Assessor has determined the apportionment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Contributions Assessor has determined how the defendants share liability for your damages
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I bound by the bar on challenging the Assessor` MEANS
    IF `a Contributions Assessor has determined the apportionment`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 52(9)(a) "is not binding on the plaintiff"
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (no determination)

§§ `cl 41 -- costs for issues and facts left in dispute`

-- cl 41(1) a party that includes in its statement of issues an issue "not determined in favour of the party and that the
-- Tribunal determines was unreasonably left in dispute ... is liable to pay the costs of any other party ..., assessed on
-- an indemnity basis, that are occasioned by establishing" it. (3) an issue later admitted counts as not determined in
-- the party's favour. (4) the same for a fact disputed and later proved or admitted.
@export Could I have to pay another party's costs on the indemnity basis for an issue or fact I left in dispute (cl 41)?
GIVEN `I put the issue or fact in dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you included the issue in your statement of issues in dispute, or disputed the fact
      `it went against me or I later admitted it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the issue was not decided in your favour (or you later admitted it), or the fact was proved or you admitted it
      `the Tribunal determines it was unreasonably left in dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal determines that it was unreasonably left in dispute
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could I pay indemnity costs for leaving it in dispute` MEANS
    IF `I put the issue or fact in dispute` AND `it went against me or I later admitted it` AND `the Tribunal determines it was unreasonably left in dispute`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- cl 41(1), (4) "If ... the party is liable": the condition fails

§§ `cl 67(3)(g) -- after the process: my statement of particulars as my affidavit`

-- cl 67(3)(f) the plaintiff may file an affidavit (only on issues that remain in dispute) and may rely on the statement of
-- particulars; (g) "the Tribunal cannot require the plaintiff to file an affidavit if the plaintiff elects to rely on the
-- plaintiff's statement of particulars served under Division 3 ... as the plaintiff's affidavit".
@export My asbestos claim has come back to the Tribunal after the claims resolution process: can the Tribunal require me to file an affidavit (cl 67(3)(g))?
GIVEN `my claim came back to the Tribunal under clause 67` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim completed the process unsettled, left it by agreement, was removed for a failure to comply, or ended under clause 58(1)
      `I elect to rely on my statement of particulars as my affidavit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you elect to rely on your statement of particulars (or parts of it) as your affidavit
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Tribunal require me to file an affidavit` MEANS
    IF `my claim came back to the Tribunal under clause 67` AND `I elect to rely on my statement of particulars as my affidavit`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- "the Tribunal cannot require"
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 67(3)(g) is silent where you do not so elect)

§§ `cl 18(2) -- after the plaintiff's death`

-- cl 11(1) "authorised representative of a deceased plaintiff means-- (a) the person who represented the plaintiff in the
-- proceedings for the claim, or (b) if the plaintiff was not represented--the plaintiff's legal personal
-- representative." cl 18(2) "If the plaintiff dies, the authorised representative of the deceased plaintiff must notify
-- the following persons in writing of the plaintiff's death (and the date of that death) as soon as practicable after
-- becoming aware of the plaintiff's death (unless the death occurred before 12 March 2007)-- (a) each defendant, (b) the
-- registrar, (c) if a mediator has been appointed, the mediator." (1) the process is suspended until the Tribunal grants
-- leave to substitute the plaintiff or add a compensation to relatives claim.
@export The plaintiff in an asbestos claim has died: am I the one who must notify each defendant, the registrar and any mediator of the death, as soon as practicable (cl 11, 18(2))?
GIVEN `I represented the plaintiff in the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were the plaintiff's representative in the proceedings (for example their solicitor)
      `the plaintiff was represented by someone` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone represented the plaintiff in the proceedings
      `I am the plaintiff's legal personal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the plaintiff's executor or administrator
      `the plaintiff died before 12 March 2007` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the plaintiff died before 12 March 2007
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I notify the plaintiff's death` MEANS
    IF `the plaintiff died before 12 March 2007`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- the "unless" is met
    ELSE IF `I represented the plaintiff in the proceedings`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE IF (NOT `the plaintiff was represented by someone`) AND `I am the plaintiff's legal personal representative`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE  -- not the "authorised representative" the "means" definition names
